{
  "evidence": "- TEXT_E: Increase NHS spending every year with at least £8bn by 2020; mental health parity; integrate health and social care; cap residential care costs; same-day GP for over-75s. Free 30 hours of childcare; protect per-pupil funding; 'Tax‑Free Minimum Wage' law; raise Personal Allowance; create 2m jobs and 3m apprenticeships; crack down on tax evasion. Victims' Law; protect pensioners (triple lock); Help to Buy/Starter Homes; maintain 0.7% aid. Controlled immigration and national security; renew Trident; EU renegotiation with referendum (not immediate exit).\n- TEXT_D: £12bn extra for the NHS; £5.2bn for social care; 8,000 more GPs, 20,000 nurses; £1.5bn for mental health/dementia; carers’ allowance boost; Dignity Code. Scrap bedroom tax; decriminalise TV licence non-payment; proportional voting and citizen referendums. Restrict benefits/NHS and social housing to citizens or 5-year contributors; five-year moratorium on unskilled immigration; 'prefer British workers'. Leave the EU and ECHR; cut aid to 0.2% GNI; abolish inheritance tax; repeal the Climate Change Act and end green levies.",
  "reasoning": "Both texts strongly address Care/Harm via substantial NHS and social-care commitments, but TEXT_E couples this with broader equality and opportunity measures (free childcare, protected school funding, maintained aid) and proportionality cues about rewarding work without heavily excluding out-groups. TEXT_D places heavier emphasis on sovereignty and in‑group loyalty (Brexit, restricting welfare/NHS/social housing to citizens/contributors, immigration moratorium) and adds equity-reducing steps (aid cuts, abolishing inheritance tax), which misaligns with the profile’s high Equality and only moderate Loyalty priorities.",
  "confidence": "medium",
  "winner": "TEXT_E"
}
